  message: "PROBE DONE — the item names ONE shape; there are at least THREE and they are one\nbug. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/pokam_scala_09102026/PROBE_RESULT.md\n(probe.py, probe2.py, probe2_src/).\n\nThe filed \"inline-qualified annotation\" miss reproduces. It is not the family.\nDiscriminating pairs, each differing in exactly one thing, WITH the imports the\nmodule slot needs (my first pass omitted them, so even the working shape read\n`external` and the control proved nothing):\n\n  A1 annotation, imported bare name   val x: File            hint File    slot java.io.File\n  A2 annotation, inline-qualified     val x: java.io.File    hint None    slot external\n  B1 new, imported bare name          new File(\"p\")          hint File    slot java.io.File\n  B2 new, inline-qualified            new java.io.File(\"p\")  hint None    slot external\n  C1 annotation, GENERIC APPLIED      val x: Buffer[String]  hint None    slot external\n  C2 annotation, bare generic base    val x: Buffer          hint Buffer  slot scala.collection.mutable.Buffer\n  D1 parameter annotation, bare       def run(x: File)       hint File    slot java.io.File\n  E1 inferred from a call             val x = mk()           hint None    slot external\n\nTWO THINGS THE FILING DOES NOT CONTAIN:\n\n(1) **It is not the annotation path.** `new java.io.File(\"p\")` loses the hint too\n(B2) while `new File(\"p\")` keeps it (B1) — the same qualified-name blindness sits\non the `new` branch, which this item lists as one of the three shapes that WORK.\nA fix scoped to the val-annotation branch would leave B2 broken AND WOULD LOOK\nLIKE A FIX.\n\n(2) **A GENERIC APPLIED TYPE loses the hint (C1) while its bare base keeps it\n(C2).** `List[String]`, `Option[X]`, `Future[X]`, `Seq[X]`, `Map[K,V]` are\npervasive in idiomatic Scala and are plausibly a far LARGER population than\ninline-qualified names, which are rare in code that has an import block. This\nshape is not mentioned in the filing at all.\n\nMECHANISM: the hint is lost whenever the type node is anything other than a bare\n`type_identifier` — qualified or generic-applied, on either the annotation path\nor the `new` path. One structural fix at the type-node extraction, not three\nspecial cases.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and I am not going to let the probe stand in for it: a probe\nsays a shape MISSES, never how OFTEN it occurs. This item's acceptance metric is\nthe HINTED SHARE on sbt/lila (baseline 10.1% / 4.8%, sigog_scala_slot_09092026/\nPLAN.md §2), and that is what has to move. Which of the two shapes does the work\nis exactly the question a headline pools, so it gets pre-registered before the\nfix. And a moved hint is necessary, not sufficient (L5): the sigog record already\nwarns that String/Boolean/Thread are Scala's implicit imports, appear in no\nimport statement, and can never qualify to a module."  # 7f16
